ince $A$ has $9$ elements, we must have $|A|=9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181583/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181583"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-181578/field-1/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" :answer/id #uuid "2cb82449-80d9-5af1-a669-dc755c417dce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181578/field-1/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "02f706c8-8689-55bc-8338-58e4a6b8fa5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181578/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "12c8add7-ed5d-5b9c-a8aa-19bdb2e78490" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-181578/field-1/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" :answer-field/choices ["q-181578/field-1/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" "q-181578/field-1/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181578" :question/id #uuid "5e04b91c-9f76-5be0-b04c-887bec843436" :question/math-academy-id "q-181578" :question/problem "If $A={3,18,108,648,…,181\\,398\\,528},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,18,\\,108,\\,648,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =6 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{108}{18} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({6}^{n-1})=\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{N} & =181\\,398\\,528 \\\\ {6}^{N} & =362\\,797\\,056 \\\\ N & ={log}_{6}⁡(362\\,797\\,056) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(362\\,797\\,056)}{{log}_{10}⁡(6)} \\\\ N & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 11}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $11$ elements, we must have $|A|=11.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181578/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181578"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-181576/field-1/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" :answer/id #uuid "b34cd758-ad00-511f-b62f-2288203de635" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181576/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b2196f8-084b-5bf3-a44f-def1c362bf61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-181576/field-1/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" :answer-field/choices ["q-181576/field-1/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181576" :question/id #uuid "b7c6f1d2-1982-573c-ba1d-3b71c29477d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-181576" :question/problem "If $A={2,10,50,250,…,2\\,441\\,406\\,250},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,50,\\,250,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{10} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=2({5}^{n-1})=\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{N} & =2\\,441\\,406\\,250 \\\\ {5}^{N} & =6\\,103\\,515\\,625 \\\\ N & ={log}_{5}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625)}{{log}_{10}⁡(5)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181576/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181576"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13148"] :question/problem "If $A = \\{1, 1, 2,1, 3,3\\}$, then what is $\\left|A\\right|?$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n1,\\qquad 2, \\qquad 3\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $A$ has $3$ elements, its cardinality is $|A| = 3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13149"] :question/problem "What is the cardinal number of $X = \\{ a, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, b \\}?$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X{:}$\n\n$$\na, \\qquad \\emptyset, \\qquad \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\qquad \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\qquad b\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\n- $\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}$ and $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$ each count as single, distinct elements of $X.$\n\nSince $X$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is $|X| = 5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13169"] :question/problem "If $A = \\{10, 19, 28,\\ldots,865\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n10, \\quad 19, \\quad 28,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =9 \\\\  & =19-10 \\\\  & =28-19 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =10+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={9n+1:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9N+1 & =865 \\\\ 9N & =864 \\\\ N & =\\frac{864}{9} \\\\ N & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{9n+1: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 96  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $96$ elements, we must have $|A| = 96.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13172"] :question/problem "If $A = \\{3, 21, 147,1\\,029,\\ldots,5\\,931\\,980\\,229\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{21} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} & =5\\,931\\,980\\,229 \\\\ {7}^{N} & =13\\,841\\,287\\,201 \\\\ N & ={log}_{7}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201)}{{log}_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have $|A| = 12.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
